# CRAYON Brand Narrative

**Version:** 1.0
**Status:** Canonical brand foundation

## 1. Brand

# CRAYON

## Create. Run. Automate. Your Own Network.

CRAYON is a creative infrastructure platform for people who build things.

Build an app. Run a service. Launch a bot. Connect your data. Automate the boring parts. Turn an idea into something that actually works.

You bring the idea.

**CRAYON makes it run.**

---

## 2. The Core Idea

### MAKE SOMETHING.

The cloud was built for infrastructure.

CRAYON is built for people.

You should not need to become a cloud expert before you can build something real. You should be able to start with an idea, bring your code, and make it live.

Create it.

Run it.

Automate it.

Make it yours.

---

## 3. Positioning

CRAYON is not positioned as a generic hosting company, a Cloudflare panel, a Docker dashboard, an AI chatbot, or a Vercel clone.

CRAYON is:

> **A creative infrastructure platform for building, running, and automating software.**

The emotional version is:

> **CRAYON is where ideas become running systems.**

The product promise is:

> **Make it run.**

---

## 4. CREATE

Everything begins with something someone wants to make.

A website.

An API.

A bot.

An AI agent.

A workflow.

A service.

A strange experiment that may or may not work.

CRAYON is built for all of it.

> **Make something.**

---

## 5. RUN

Code only matters when it runs.

CRAYON makes deployment and runtime feel like part of building rather than a separate infrastructure project.

Applications, APIs, containers, workers, services, domains, logs, health, and runtime resources belong to the same operational surface.

Build it locally.

Ship it.

Watch it come alive.

> **Make it run.**

---

## 6. AUTOMATE

Do not do the same thing twice.

Connect events, APIs, applications, AI, messages, schedules, and actions into workflows that keep working when nobody is watching.

Let machines handle the boring parts.

Build the interesting ones.

> **Make it automatic.**

---

## 7. YOUR OWN NETWORK

A network is more than an IP address.

It is the connected world around your software: applications, APIs, data, storage, domains, bots, AI agents, automations, services, and rules.

CRAYON gives those pieces a place to connect.

Your software.

Your data.

Your workflows.

Your rules.

> **Your own network.**

---

## 8. THE HUMAN SIDE

### YOUR CODE. YOUR RULES. YOUR MESS.

Software is rarely clean while it is being made.

Ideas are messy.

Experiments fail.

Deployments break.

Logs get weird.

Bots reconnect.

Someone ships something at 2 AM and somehow it works.

That is fine.

Your infrastructure does not have to look perfect.

**It just has to work.**

This is a core CRAYON belief: serious technology does not have to feel sterile.

---

## 9. BUILD WEIRD THINGS

CRAYON is for developers, builders, startups, businesses, agencies, automation builders, AI experimenters, and anyone who looks at a blank screen and thinks:

> **I could build that.**

Build something useful.

Build something strange.

Build something nobody expected.

Then make it run.

---

## 10. THE CRAYON ATTITUDE

CRAYON is serious about software.

It is not serious about pretending software has to be boring.

Ship it.

Break it.

Fix it.

Automate it.

Try again.

Keep building.

---

## 11. Official Meaning

**CRAYON — Create. Run. Automate. Your Own Network.**

This is the official interpretation and should be used in formal product, technical, and business contexts.

It maps directly to the product model:

```text
CREATE
  Apps · APIs · AI · Projects

RUN
  Deployments · Containers · Workers · Runtime

AUTOMATE
  Workflows · Jobs · AI Agents · Bots

YOUR OWN NETWORK
  Domains · Storage · Data · Secrets · Integrations
```

---

## 12. Unofficial Meaning

**CRAYON — Create, Run And Yell Online, Now.**

This is intentionally unofficial.

It is CRAYON's underground meaning: a little reckless, a little funny, and very builder-minded.

It communicates the attitude:

> **Stop overthinking. Build it. Run it. Ship it.**

The unofficial meaning can appear in brand lore, easter eggs, social content, launch material, merch, or places where CRAYON is allowed to have a personality.

---

## 13. Tone of Voice

CRAYON should sound human, confident, direct, technical, playful, and slightly rebellious.

### Prefer

> **Ship it.**

> **Yep. It's alive.**

> **Make it run.**

> **WhatsApp is alive.**

> **It died. We brought it back.**

### Avoid

> Deployment completed successfully.

> Configure your production application environment.

> Next-generation cloud-native infrastructure orchestration platform.

Personality should never reduce clarity. Technical documentation remains precise even when the surrounding brand is playful.

---

## 14. Brand Personality

CRAYON combines:

```text
PLAYFUL
+
REBELLIOUS
+
TECHNICAL
+
HUMAN
+
MINIMAL
```

It is not:

```text
CHILDISH
CARTOONISH
CHAOTIC
CORPORATE-JARGON-HEAVY
CYBERPUNK-FOR-THE-SAKE-OF-CYBERPUNK
```

The crayon metaphor represents human creativity applied to serious infrastructure.

---

## 15. Visual Philosophy

CRAYON's visual language is **Crayon Infrastructure**.

Target ratio:

**90% clean premium SaaS + 10% crayon/grunge personality.**

The aesthetic combines:

```text
PAPER
+
INK
+
MARKER
+
TECH
```

Use crayon language for:

- hand-drawn underlines
- annotations
- arrows
- highlights
- status accents
- micro-interactions

Do not turn every UI component into a doodle.

CRAYON should feel expressive without becoming childish.

---

## 16. Master Brand Statements

### Brand

**CRAYON**

### Official meaning

**Create. Run. Automate. Your Own Network.**

### Core promise

**Make it run.**

### Emotional positioning

**Where ideas become running systems.**

### Brand philosophy

**Your code. Your rules. Your mess.**

### Unofficial attitude

**Create, Run And Yell Online, Now.**

---

## 17. Master Paragraph

> **CRAYON is a creative infrastructure platform for people who build things. Create applications, run them anywhere, automate the boring parts, and connect everything into your own network. We do not care if your infrastructure is perfectly tidy. We care that it works.**

---

## 18. Homepage Core Copy

### MAKE SOMETHING.

Make it run.

Make it automate.

Make it yours.

**CRAYON is where ideas become running systems.**

**Create. Run. Automate. Your Own Network.**

---

## 19. Brand Rule

CRAYON should always feel like the intersection of:

```text
SERIOUS ENGINEERING
        ×
HUMAN CREATIVITY
        ×
BUILDER ATTITUDE
```

The product should be powerful enough for production, simple enough to start quickly, and distinctive enough to be remembered.

**Make it run.**

# CRAYON — Application Shell Showcase v1.0

## Status

**Version:** 1.0  
**Role:** Canonical authenticated application-shell UX specification  
**Brand:** CRAYON  
**Official meaning:** Create. Run. Automate. Your Own Network.  
**Core promise:** Make it run.

---

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the canonical authenticated CRAYON application shell before page-level implementation. Every dashboard, resource detail page, workspace, and operational surface must fit inside this shell unless an explicit product decision creates a different surface.

The shell is designed around user intent rather than provider terminology.

```text
CREATE → RUN → AUTOMATE → CONNECT → OBSERVE → IMPROVE
```

The visual system follows the CRAYON principle:

```text
CLEAN SYSTEM + HUMAN MARKS + TECHNICAL PRECISION
```

Target ratio:

```text
90% professional SaaS
10% crayon / grunge personality
```

---

## 2. Canonical Desktop Frame

```text
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ CRAYON       [ Project ▾ ] [ Environment ▾ ]        ⌘K   +   ?   ● avatar   │
├────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                │                                                            │
│ OVERVIEW       │  Make something.                                          │
│                │  Make it run.                                             │
│ CREATE         │                                                            │
│ Applications   │  ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────┐ ┌─────────┐ │
│ Templates      │  │ 12 Apps    │ │ 8 Running  │ │ 3 Deploys  │ │ 2.4 GB  │ │
│ Projects       │  │            │ │            │ │            │ │ storage │ │
│                │  └────────────┘ └────────────┘ └────────────┘ └─────────┘ │
│ RUN            │                                                            │
│ Deployments    │  NEEDS ATTENTION                                          │
│ Containers     │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│ Workers        │  │ api-prod    BUILD FAILED          View logs →       │  │
│ Jobs           │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                │                                                            │
│ AUTOMATE       │  DEPLOYMENTS                                               │
│ Workflows      │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│ AI Agents      │  │ ● shop-api       LIVE        2m ago                 │  │
│ WhatsApp       │  │ ● storefront     LIVE        7m ago                 │  │
│                │  │ ● wa-sales       CONNECTED   12m ago                │  │
│ NETWORK        │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│ Domains        │                                                            │
│ Storage        │  ┌──────────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────────┐ │
│ Data           │  │ WHATSAPP             │ │ AUTOMATIONS                │ │
│ Secrets        │  │ WA-SALES-01          │ │ 37 active                  │ │
│                │  │ ● CONNECTED          │ │ 1,284 runs                 │ │
│ OBSERVE        │  │ 12,492 messages      │ │ 99.2% success              │ │
│ Logs           │  │ AI ON                │ │                            │ │
│ Metrics        │  └──────────────────────┘ └────────────────────────────┘ │
│ Usage          │                                                            │
│                │  RESOURCE USAGE                                            │
│ ACCOUNT        │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│ Billing        │  │ CPU     ███████░░   Memory  █████░░░░░             │  │
│ Team           │  │ Storage ████░░░░░   Requests ██████░░░             │  │
│ Settings       │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                │                                                            │
│                │  RECENT ACTIVITY                                           │
│                │  2m  shop-api deployed successfully                       │
│                │  8m  WA-SALES-01 reconnected                             │
│                │  11m domain added to storefront                          │
│                │                                                            │
└────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

This is a structural reference, not a fixed pixel mockup. Actual implementation must remain responsive and accessible.

---

## 3. Shell Regions

The shell has six persistent regions.

### 3.1 Global Header

```text
[CRAYON] [Organization ▾] [Project ▾] [Environment ▾]
                         [Global Search / ⌘K] [Create +] [?] [Notifications] [Avatar]
```

Responsibilities:

- current context
- global search
- command palette
- create action
- help/documentation
- notifications
- profile/account access

The header must remain visually quiet. It is infrastructure, not hero content.

### 3.2 Primary Sidebar

The sidebar owns high-level information architecture.

```text
OVERVIEW

CREATE
  Applications
  Templates
  Projects

RUN
  Deployments
  Containers
  Workers
  Jobs

AUTOMATE
  Workflows
  AI Agents
  WhatsApp

NETWORK
  Domains
  Storage
  Data
  Secrets

OBSERVE
  Logs
  Metrics
  Usage

ACCOUNT
  Billing
  Team
  Settings
```

Rules:

- maximum one active item
- section labels are quiet and compact
- no nested tree deeper than necessary in the primary sidebar
- resource-specific navigation appears inside detail pages
- destructive or account-sensitive actions are not exposed as primary navigation shortcuts

### 3.3 Context Bar

Project and environment are first-class context.

```text
Project: storefront ▾
Environment: production ▾
```

Context switching must update the content scope without silently changing organization.

### 3.4 Main Workspace

The main workspace is where intent is executed. Each page must declare one primary action.

### 3.5 Utility Rail / Drawer

Optional on wide screens for contextual details:

```text
activity
resource metadata
quick actions
notifications
```

It must collapse on smaller widths.

### 3.6 Global Feedback Layer

Transient feedback must appear consistently:

```text
success toast
warning toast
error toast
progress notification
background job indicator
```

Important state changes should remain discoverable in activity/event history rather than disappearing after a toast.

---

## 4. Context Switching

CRAYON operates with this hierarchy:

```text
Organization
   ↓
Project
   ↓
Environment
   ↓
Resource
```

The current scope must always be visible.

Changing project or environment must:

1. update resource lists,
2. update metrics and usage,
3. update navigation where relevant,
4. preserve safe return navigation,
5. never mutate data merely by switching context.

Production environments should have a visually distinct but restrained indicator.

---

## 5. Global Search

Search is available from the header and command palette.

Search scope:

```text
Applications
Deployments
Containers
Workers
Jobs
WhatsApp instances
Workflows
AI Agents
Domains
Logs
Events
```

Result rows should include:

```text
resource name
resource type
status
project/environment
recent activity
```

Keyboard:

```text
⌘K / Ctrl+K → open
↑ ↓           → navigate
Enter         → open
Esc           → close
```

---

## 6. Command Palette

The command palette is a primary control surface.

Example commands:

```text
Create application
Deploy application
Create container
Create WhatsApp instance
Create workflow
Add domain
Open logs
Restart resource
View usage
Open billing
Search resources
```

Safe actions may execute directly.

Destructive actions route through explicit confirmation:

```text
Delete application?
This will remove X resources.
[Cancel] [Delete]
```

---

## 7. Create Action

The global `+ Create` action should expose a short list of high-value intents:

```text
Application
Container
Worker
Workflow
AI Agent
WhatsApp
Project
Domain
```

The create menu must not expose raw provider primitives unless advanced mode is enabled.

---

## 8. Dashboard Composition

The dashboard follows attention order:

```text
1. Needs attention
2. What's running
3. What changed
4. What's deploying
5. Resource usage
6. Recent activity
```

Recommended blocks:

```text
NEEDS ATTENTION
HEALTH SNAPSHOT
ACTIVE DEPLOYMENTS
APPLICATIONS
WHATSAPP
AUTOMATIONS
RESOURCE USAGE
RECENT ACTIVITY
```

Do not fill the dashboard with charts simply because charts are available.

---

## 9. Status Language

Status must be explicit and consistent.

```text
● RUNNING
● CONNECTED
● HEALTHY
○ STOPPED
◐ BUILDING
◐ DEPLOYING
◌ RECONNECTING
△ DEGRADED
× FAILED
```

Status colors are semantic tokens, not decorative brand colors.

Important states also receive text, icon, and accessible labels so color is never the only signal.

---

## 10. Application Detail Shell

Every major resource follows this grammar:

```text
[ Resource name ]   [status]          [Primary action]
[subtext / identifier / environment]

Overview | Activity | Configuration | Usage

┌─────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────────┐
│ Current state       │ │ Key metrics         │
└─────────────────────┘ └─────────────────────┘

Activity / logs / deployments

Configuration / variables / networking
```

Primary action examples:

```text
Deploy
Restart
Connect
Run
Enable
```

---

## 11. Deployment Detail

Deployment detail is optimized for diagnosis.

```text
BUILD → RELEASE → START → HEALTH CHECK
```

The page should answer:

```text
What version?
What happened?
Where did it fail?
What changed?
Can I retry?
```

Failed deployment priority:

```text
Failure reason
↓
Relevant log excerpt
↓
Likely cause
↓
Suggested action
↓
Retry
```

---

## 12. WhatsApp Detail

WhatsApp/Baileys is a flagship workload.

```text
WA-SALES-01                         ● CONNECTED

[QR / Pairing]

Messages        12,492
Automations        37
AI                 ON
Uptime        7d 12h

Overview | Messages | Automations | Webhooks | Logs | Usage
```

Connection states must distinguish:

```text
PAIRING
CONNECTED
RECONNECTING
DISCONNECTED
AUTH_REQUIRED
FAILED
```

The UI should explain what the user needs to do next.

For a pairing flow:

```text
Generate QR
      ↓
Scan with WhatsApp
      ↓
Waiting for device
      ↓
Connected
```

---

## 13. Automation Detail

Workflow UI uses a readable sequence rather than a dense diagram by default.

```text
TRIGGER
   ↓
CONTEXT
   ↓
DECISION
   ↓
ACTION
   ↓
OBSERVATION
```

Example:

```text
WhatsApp message
      ↓
AI classify intent
      ↓
Create lead
      ↓
Send product information
      ↓
Create invoice
      ↓
Notify operator
```

Advanced graph view may be available, but the linear representation remains the default for simple workflows.

---

## 14. Logs and Terminal Surfaces

Logs must feel technical without becoming visually hostile.

```text
19:41:02  INFO  deploy started
19:41:08  INFO  image pulled
19:41:14  INFO  container started
19:41:18  INFO  health check passed
19:41:18  INFO  deployment live
```

Requirements:

- monospace typography
- copy action
- timestamps
- severity
- filtering
- search
- pause/resume streaming
- clear distinction between live and historical logs
- no fake streaming animations

---

## 15. Responsive Behavior

### Desktop

```text
sidebar + full workspace
```

### Tablet

```text
compact sidebar
contextual drawers
fewer dashboard columns
```

### Mobile

```text
top bar
bottom/slide navigation
stacked cards
full-screen resource detail
```

The product must not simply shrink desktop layouts. Content hierarchy changes by viewport.

---

## 16. Crayon / Grunge Placement Rules

Crayon personality is intentional and sparse.

### Allowed

```text
marker underline
hand-drawn arrow
small handwritten note
organic border accent
paper texture
subtle scribble around a key action
```

### Not allowed

```text
full hand-drawn cards
doodles everywhere
cartoon mascots
random stickers on every component
rough typography for primary UI text
visual noise behind dense tables
```

Recommended visual split:

```text
Professional interface  ██████████████████ 90%
Crayon personality      ██ 10%
```

---

## 17. Accessibility

The shell must target WCAG 2.2 AA behavior.

Required:

- keyboard access to all primary controls
- visible focus state
- semantic landmarks
- accessible names for icon-only buttons
- text alternatives for status indicators
- reduced-motion support
- readable contrast
- no color-only state communication
- logical tab order

Crayon texture must never reduce text contrast or obscure focus indicators.

---

## 18. Empty / Loading / Error States

Every first-class page must implement:

```text
EMPTY
LOADING
SUCCESS
DEGRADED
ERROR
NO PERMISSION
```

Empty state example:

```text
Nothing here yet.

Create your first application.

[Create application]
```

Error state example:

```text
Something broke.

We could not load this deployment.

[Retry] [Open status]
```

Copy must describe the situation instead of using placeholder language such as “Lorem ipsum” or generic “Something went wrong” without recovery guidance.

---

## 19. Navigation Invariants

The shell must preserve:

```text
current organization
current project
current environment
current resource
current tab
```

Deep links must be stable.

Browser back/forward must work.

Refresh must preserve the current route and context.

Command palette navigation must produce normal routable URLs.

---

## 20. Performance Rules

The shell should prioritize perceived responsiveness.

- render navigation immediately
- load large datasets incrementally
- stream logs only when requested
- avoid loading every dashboard widget before first paint
- retain previous data during safe refetches
- debounce global search
- virtualize long tables/log streams
- isolate high-frequency metrics from the rest of the page

Decorative animation must never compete with operational updates.

---

## 21. Implementation Contract

The shell should be implemented as reusable primitives rather than page-specific markup.

Suggested component layers:

```text
Shell
├── GlobalHeader
├── ContextSwitcher
├── Sidebar
├── CommandPalette
├── CreateMenu
├── Notifications
├── WorkspaceFrame
├── ResourceHeader
├── ResourceTabs
├── StatusBadge
├── MetricCard
├── ActivityFeed
├── DataTable
├── LogViewer
├── EmptyState
├── LoadingState
├── ErrorState
└── CrayonPrimitives
    ├── CrayonUnderline
    ├── CrayonArrow
    ├── CrayonNote
    ├── PaperSurface
    └── BlueprintGrid
```

No feature team should invent another shell pattern for an individual module.

---

## 22. Canonical Example Copy

### Dashboard

```text
Make something.
Make it run.
```

### Healthy resource

```text
Yep. It's alive.
```

### Deployment

```text
Ship it.
```

### Reconnecting

```text
Connection dropped. We're bringing it back.
```

### Failed deployment

```text
It broke.
Here's why.
```

Brand personality may appear in these microcopy moments, but technical details remain explicit.

---

## 23. Acceptance Criteria

The application shell is considered complete only when:

```text
[ ] global context is always visible
[ ] sidebar matches IA
[ ] command palette works
[ ] create menu works
[ ] project/environment switching is safe
[ ] deep links work
[ ] keyboard navigation works
[ ] loading/error/empty states exist
[ ] status system is consistent
[ ] resource detail grammar is reusable
[ ] deployment diagnosis is supported
[ ] WhatsApp pairing surface is defined
[ ] logs are usable
[ ] responsive behavior is implemented
[ ] reduced-motion/accessibility requirements are handled
[ ] crayon styling does not reduce usability
```

---

## 24. Next Surface

After this shell is implemented and visually verified, the next canonical showcase is:

```text
CRAYON DASHBOARD v1.0
```

It should use the real shell, real component grammar, real state language, and the first production-oriented resource examples. After dashboard approval, build the first monetizable journey:

```text
WHATSAPP / BAILEYS
CREATE → QR → CONNECT → AUTOMATE → OBSERVE
```

