│                │  8m  WA-SALES-01 reconnected                             │
│                │  11m domain added to storefront                          │
│                │                                                            │
└────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

This is a structural reference, not a fixed pixel mockup. Actual implementation must remain responsive and accessible.

---

## 3. Shell Regions

The shell has six persistent regions.

### 3.1 Global Header

```text
[CRAYON] [Organization ▾] [Project ▾] [Environment ▾]
                         [Global Search / ⌘K] [Create +] [?] [Notifications] [Avatar]
```

Responsibilities:

- current context
- global search
- command palette
- create action
- help/documentation
- notifications
- profile/account access

The header must remain visually quiet. It is infrastructure, not hero content.

### 3.2 Primary Sidebar

The sidebar owns high-level information architecture.

```text
OVERVIEW

CREATE
  Applications
  Templates
  Projects

RUN
  Deployments
  Containers
  Workers
  Jobs

AUTOMATE
  Workflows
  AI Agents
  WhatsApp

NETWORK
  Domains
  Storage
  Data
  Secrets

OBSERVE
  Logs
  Metrics
  Usage

ACCOUNT
  Billing
  Team
  Settings
```

Rules:

- maximum one active item
- section labels are quiet and compact
- no nested tree deeper than necessary in the primary sidebar
- resource-specific navigation appears inside detail pages
- destructive or account-sensitive actions are not exposed as primary navigation shortcuts

### 3.3 Context Bar

Project and environment are first-class context.

```text
Project: storefront ▾
Environment: production ▾
```

Context switching must update the content scope without silently changing organization.

### 3.4 Main Workspace

The main workspace is where intent is executed. Each page must declare one primary action.

### 3.5 Utility Rail / Drawer

Optional on wide screens for contextual details:

```text
activity
resource metadata
quick actions
notifications
```

It must collapse on smaller widths.

### 3.6 Global Feedback Layer

Transient feedback must appear consistently:

```text
success toast
warning toast
error toast
progress notification
background job indicator
```

Important state changes should remain discoverable in activity/event history rather than disappearing after a toast.

---

## 4. Context Switching

CRAYON operates with this hierarchy:

```text
Organization
   ↓
Project
   ↓
Environment
   ↓
Resource
```

The current scope must always be visible.

Changing project or environment must:

1. update resource lists,
2. update metrics and usage,
3. update navigation where relevant,
4. preserve safe return navigation,
5. never mutate data merely by switching context.

Production environments should have a visually distinct but restrained indicator.

---

## 5. Global Search

Search is available from the header and command palette.

Search scope:

```text
Applications
Deployments
Containers
Workers
Jobs
WhatsApp instances
Workflows
AI Agents
Domains
Logs
Events
```

Result rows should include:

```text
resource name
resource type
status
project/environment
recent activity
```

Keyboard:

```text
⌘K / Ctrl+K → open
↑ ↓           → navigate
Enter         → open
Esc           → close
```

---

## 6. Command Palette

The command palette is a primary control surface.

Example commands:

```text
Create application
Deploy application
Create container
Create WhatsApp instance
Create workflow
Add domain
Open logs
Restart resource
View usage
Open billing
Search resources
```

Safe actions may execute directly.

Destructive actions route through explicit confirmation:

```text
Delete application?
This will remove X resources.
[Cancel] [Delete]
```

---

## 7. Create Action

The global `+ Create` action should expose a short list of high-value intents:

```text
Application
Container
Worker
Workflow
AI Agent
WhatsApp
Project
Domain
```

The create menu must not expose raw provider primitives unless advanced mode is enabled.

---

## 8. Dashboard Composition

The dashboard follows attention order:

```text
1. Needs attention
2. What's running
3. What changed
4. What's deploying
5. Resource usage
6. Recent activity
```

Recommended blocks:

```text
NEEDS ATTENTION
HEALTH SNAPSHOT
ACTIVE DEPLOYMENTS
APPLICATIONS
WHATSAPP
AUTOMATIONS
RESOURCE USAGE
RECENT ACTIVITY
```

Do not fill the dashboard with charts simply because charts are available.

---

## 9. Status Language

Status must be explicit and consistent.

```text
● RUNNING
● CONNECTED
● HEALTHY
○ STOPPED
◐ BUILDING
◐ DEPLOYING
◌ RECONNECTING
△ DEGRADED
× FAILED
```

Status colors are semantic tokens, not decorative brand colors.

Important states also receive text, icon, and accessible labels so color is never the only signal.

---

## 10. Application Detail Shell

Every major resource follows this grammar:

```text
[ Resource name ]   [status]          [Primary action]
[subtext / identifier / environment]

Overview | Activity | Configuration | Usage

┌─────────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────────┐
│ Current state       │ │ Key metrics         │
└─────────────────────┘ └─────────────────────┘

Activity / logs / deployments

Configuration / variables / networking
```

Primary action examples:

```text
Deploy
Restart
Connect
Run
Enable
```

---

## 11. Deployment Detail

Deployment detail is optimized for diagnosis.

```text
BUILD → RELEASE → START → HEALTH CHECK
```

The page should answer:

```text
What version?
What happened?
Where did it fail?
What changed?
Can I retry?
```

Failed deployment priority:

```text
Failure reason
↓
Relevant log excerpt
↓
Likely cause
↓
Suggested action
↓
Retry
```

---

## 12. WhatsApp Detail

WhatsApp/Baileys is a flagship workload.

```text
WA-SALES-01                         ● CONNECTED

[QR / Pairing]

Messages        12,492
Automations        37
AI                 ON
Uptime        7d 12h

Overview | Messages | Automations | Webhooks | Logs | Usage
```

Connection states must distinguish:

```text
PAIRING
CONNECTED
RECONNECTING
DISCONNECTED
AUTH_REQUIRED
FAILED
```

The UI should explain what the user needs to do next.

For a pairing flow:

```text
Generate QR
      ↓
Scan with WhatsApp
      ↓
Waiting for device
      ↓
Connected
```

---

## 13. Automation Detail

Workflow UI uses a readable sequence rather than a dense diagram by default.

```text
TRIGGER
   ↓
CONTEXT
   ↓
DECISION
   ↓
ACTION
   ↓
OBSERVATION
```

Example:

```text
WhatsApp message
      ↓
AI classify intent
      ↓
Create lead
      ↓
Send product information
      ↓
Create invoice
      ↓
Notify operator
```

Advanced graph view may be available, but the linear representation remains the default for simple workflows.

---

## 14. Logs and Terminal Surfaces

Logs must feel technical without becoming visually hostile.

```text
19:41:02  INFO  deploy started
19:41:08  INFO  image pulled
19:41:14  INFO  container started
19:41:18  INFO  health check passed
19:41:18  INFO  deployment live
```

Requirements:

- monospace typography
- copy action
- timestamps
- severity
- filtering
- search
- pause/resume streaming
- clear distinction between live and historical logs
- no fake streaming animations

---

## 15. Responsive Behavior

### Desktop

```text
sidebar + full workspace
```

### Tablet

```text
compact sidebar
contextual drawers
fewer dashboard columns
```

### Mobile

```text
top bar
bottom/slide navigation
stacked cards
full-screen resource detail
```

The product must not simply shrink desktop layouts. Content hierarchy changes by viewport.

---

## 16. Crayon / Grunge Placement Rules

Crayon personality is intentional and sparse.

### Allowed

```text
marker underline
hand-drawn arrow
small handwritten note
organic border accent
paper texture
subtle scribble around a key action
```

### Not allowed

```text
full hand-drawn cards
doodles everywhere
cartoon mascots
random stickers on every component
rough typography for primary UI text
visual noise behind dense tables
```

Recommended visual split:

```text
Professional interface  ██████████████████ 90%
Crayon personality      ██ 10%
```

---

## 17. Accessibility

The shell must target WCAG 2.2 AA behavior.

Required:

- keyboard access to all primary controls
- visible focus state
- semantic landmarks
- accessible names for icon-only buttons
- text alternatives for status indicators
- reduced-motion support
- readable contrast
- no color-only state communication
- logical tab order

Crayon texture must never reduce text contrast or obscure focus indicators.

---

## 18. Empty / Loading / Error States

Every first-class page must implement:

```text
EMPTY
LOADING
SUCCESS
DEGRADED
ERROR
NO PERMISSION
```

Empty state example:

```text
Nothing here yet.

Create your first application.

[Create application]
```

Error state example:

```text
Something broke.

We could not load this deployment.

[Retry] [Open status]
```

Copy must describe the situation instead of using placeholder language such as “Lorem ipsum” or generic “Something went wrong” without recovery guidance.

---

## 19. Navigation Invariants

The shell must preserve:

```text
current organization
current project
current environment
current resource
current tab
```

Deep links must be stable.

Browser back/forward must work.

Refresh must preserve the current route and context.

Command palette navigation must produce normal routable URLs.

---

## 20. Performance Rules

The shell should prioritize perceived responsiveness.

- render navigation immediately
- load large datasets incrementally
- stream logs only when requested
- avoid loading every dashboard widget before first paint
- retain previous data during safe refetches
- debounce global search
- virtualize long tables/log streams
- isolate high-frequency metrics from the rest of the page

Decorative animation must never compete with operational updates.

---

## 21. Implementation Contract

The shell should be implemented as reusable primitives rather than page-specific markup.

Suggested component layers:

```text
Shell
├── GlobalHeader
├── ContextSwitcher
├── Sidebar
├── CommandPalette
├── CreateMenu
├── Notifications
├── WorkspaceFrame
├── ResourceHeader
├── ResourceTabs
├── StatusBadge
├── MetricCard
├── ActivityFeed
├── DataTable
├── LogViewer
├── EmptyState
├── LoadingState
├── ErrorState
└── CrayonPrimitives
    ├── CrayonUnderline
    ├── CrayonArrow
    ├── CrayonNote
    ├── PaperSurface
    └── BlueprintGrid
```

No feature team should invent another shell pattern for an individual module.

---

## 22. Canonical Example Copy

### Dashboard

```text
Make something.
Make it run.
```

### Healthy resource

```text
Yep. It's alive.
```

### Deployment

```text
Ship it.
```

### Reconnecting

```text
Connection dropped. We're bringing it back.
```

### Failed deployment

```text
It broke.
Here's why.
```

Brand personality may appear in these microcopy moments, but technical details remain explicit.

---

## 23. Acceptance Criteria

The application shell is considered complete only when:

```text
[ ] global context is always visible
[ ] sidebar matches IA
[ ] command palette works
[ ] create menu works
[ ] project/environment switching is safe
[ ] deep links work
[ ] keyboard navigation works
[ ] loading/error/empty states exist
[ ] status system is consistent
[ ] resource detail grammar is reusable
[ ] deployment diagnosis is supported
[ ] WhatsApp pairing surface is defined
[ ] logs are usable
[ ] responsive behavior is implemented
[ ] reduced-motion/accessibility requirements are handled
[ ] crayon styling does not reduce usability
```

---

## 24. Next Surface

After this shell is implemented and visually verified, the next canonical showcase is:

```text
CRAYON DASHBOARD v1.0
```

It should use the real shell, real component grammar, real state language, and the first production-oriented resource examples. After dashboard approval, build the first monetizable journey:

```text
WHATSAPP / BAILEYS
CREATE → QR → CONNECT → AUTOMATE → OBSERVE
```

# CRAYON — Dashboard Showcase v1.0

## Status

**Version:** 1.0  
**Role:** Dashboard-specific UX specification  
**Depends on:** Brand Narrative, Product Narrative, Information Architecture, Domain/Resource Model, UI Design System, Core Component Showcase, Application Shell Showcase

This document defines only the authenticated Overview/Dashboard experience. It does not redefine global branding, navigation, reusable components, or the underlying resource model.

---

## 1. Dashboard job

The dashboard answers one question first:

> **What needs my attention right now?**

Then it answers, in order:

```text
1. WHAT NEEDS ATTENTION
2. WHAT IS RUNNING
3. WHAT CHANGED
4. WHAT IS DEPLOYING
5. WHAT IS COSTING / USING RESOURCES
6. WHAT HAPPENED RECENTLY
```

The dashboard is an operational cockpit, not an analytics wall.

---

## 2. Page anatomy

```text
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ GLOBAL HEADER                                               │
├──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│              │ PAGE HEADER                                  │
│ APPLICATION  │ project / environment context                │
│ SHELL        │                                              │
│              │ ATTENTION STRIP                              │
│              │                                              │
│              │ HEALTH SNAPSHOT                              │
│              │                                              │
│              │ ACTIVE / RECENT DEPLOYMENTS                  │
│              │                                              │
│              │ RESOURCE + AUTOMATION SUMMARY                │
│              │                                              │
│              │ RECENT ACTIVITY                              │
└──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The dashboard must remain useful at 1280px wide without requiring horizontal scrolling.

---

## 3. Page header

Primary heading:

> **Make something.**

Supporting line:

> Build, run, and automate everything that keeps your work moving.

The header shows the active project and environment using the shell's existing context controls.

Primary action:

```text
[ + Create ]
```

The Create menu exposes only contextually valid actions:

```text
Application
WhatsApp
Workflow
AI Agent
Storage
Domain
```

Do not introduce a second creation taxonomy in the dashboard.

---

## 4. Attention strip

Only render this section when actionable attention exists.

Examples:

```text
! 1 deployment failed
! WhatsApp WA-SALES is reconnecting
! Production storage is 91% full
! 1 certificate expires in 7 days
```

Each item must have:

```text
severity
short explanation
primary recovery action
optional detail link
```

Example:

```text
! shop-api deployment failed
  TypeScript build error
  [View logs] [Retry]
```

Do not use vague alerts such as `Something went wrong` when a concrete reason is available.

---

## 5. Health snapshot

Four to six compact metrics maximum.

Recommended:

```text
APPLICATIONS     12
RUNNING           8
DEPLOYING         2
FAILED            1
AUTOMATIONS      37
RUNTIME USAGE   2.4 GB
```

Metric cards communicate current state, not vanity metrics.

Each metric should support click-through to the relevant filtered resource view.

---

## 6. Active deployments

The deployment block answers:

> **What is changing right now?**

Recommended table columns:

```text
APPLICATION
STATUS
RUNTIME
VERSION / COMMIT
STARTED
DURATION
ACTION
```

Example rows:

```text
shop-api       DEPLOYING     Node.js     a82f4c     2m ago   44s
storefront     LIVE          Next.js     e91d21     8m ago   —
wa-sales       CONNECTED     Baileys     v1         2h ago   —
```

`LIVE`, `DEPLOYING`, `FAILED`, `RECONNECTING`, and `STOPPED` must be visually distinct using the established status system.

---

## 7. Applications overview

The applications block shows a compact list or grid depending on viewport.

Each application summary contains:

```text
name
status
runtime
region
last deployment
health signal
```

Primary interaction is opening the application detail page.

Secondary actions should be available through a row menu rather than visual clutter.

---

## 8. WhatsApp summary

WhatsApp has a dedicated dashboard block because it is a flagship CRAYON workload.

The block answers:

> **Are my WhatsApp connections alive and useful?**

Show at most the most relevant instances, with a link to the full WhatsApp area.

Example:

```text
WA-SALES-01     ● CONNECTED
Messages        12,492
Automations     37
AI              ON

WA-SUPPORT-01   ! RECONNECTING
Last seen       34s ago
```

If no WhatsApp instance exists, show a product-aware empty state:

```text
No WhatsApp instance yet.

Connect a number and start automating conversations.

[ Create WhatsApp ]
```

---

## 9. Automation summary

Show only operationally meaningful workflow information.

```text
ACTIVE       14
RUNNING       2
FAILED        1
PAUSED        3
```

A small recent-run list may show:

```text
Lead follow-up       SUCCESS    12s ago
Invoice reminder     SUCCESS    2m ago
WhatsApp classifier  FAILED     4m ago
```

The dashboard must never simulate real-time activity. Data shown must come from actual runtime events or explicitly indicate when it was last updated.

---

## 10. Resource usage

The default usage block is intentionally compact.

```text
CPU       ███████░░░   62%
MEMORY    █████░░░░░   48%
STORAGE   ████░░░░░░   39%
REQUESTS  ██████░░░░   57%
```

Show current period and comparison only when the comparison is meaningful.

Examples:

```text
This month
vs previous month
```

Avoid decorative charts with no operational interpretation.

---

## 11. Recent activity

The activity stream is event-oriented.

Examples:

```text
2m   shop-api deployed successfully
8m   WA-SALES received 42 messages
14m  workflow invoice-reminder completed
21m  domain api.example.com verified
43m  deployment v43 failed
```

Every item needs a timestamp and an object link.

Events must distinguish:

```text
DEPLOYMENT
RUNTIME
AUTOMATION
WHATSAPP
NETWORK
BILLING
SECURITY
```

---

## 12. Dashboard priority algorithm

The server/API should return dashboard sections in an attention-aware order, but the UI remains responsible for presentation.

Conceptual score:

```text
attention_score =
  severity
  × urgency
  × user_relevance
  × recency
```

High-scoring items appear before passive metrics.

Critical failures can temporarily replace non-actionable dashboard content at the top of the page.

---

## 13. Empty state

For a brand-new organization:

```text
MAKE SOMETHING.

Your first project is empty.
Let's put something in it.

[ Create an application ]
[ Try a template ]
```

The empty state should teach the product loop without presenting a generic illustration wall.

Secondary explanation:

```text
Create → Run → Automate
```

---

## 14. Loading state

Initial page load should use structural skeletons matching final dimensions.

Prefer:

```text
skeleton metric
skeleton table rows
skeleton activity rows
```

Do not use a full-screen spinner for ordinary dashboard loading.

For delayed sections, load independently so the dashboard becomes useful as soon as the first data arrives.

---

## 15. Error state

If one data source fails, isolate the failure.

Bad:

```text
Dashboard failed to load.
```

Preferred:

```text
Applications unavailable right now.

The rest of your dashboard is still available.

[ Retry applications ]
```

The dashboard should degrade gracefully when one adapter or provider is unavailable.

---

## 16. Refresh behavior

Use progressive refresh:

```text
Initial load
  ↓
short-lived cache
  ↓
background refresh
  ↓
visible timestamp update
```

Operations requiring immediate state should offer an explicit refresh affordance.

Long-running deployment operations should use event/polling mechanisms appropriate to the backend rather than refreshing the entire page.

---

## 17. Responsive behavior

### Desktop

Use two visual columns where space permits:

```text
primary content  ~ 2/3
secondary content ~ 1/3
```

### Tablet

Stack secondary operational summaries below primary deployments.

### Mobile

Priority order becomes:

```text
Attention
Health
Deployments
WhatsApp
Automation
Usage
Activity
```

The dashboard should not attempt to preserve the desktop grid at mobile widths.

---

## 18. Interaction rules

Dashboard cards are entry points, not mini-applications.

Use:

```text
click card → filtered resource view
click row → resource detail
primary action → immediate task
menu → secondary operations
```

Destructive operations must not be executable from passive metric cards.

Examples requiring explicit confirmation:

```text
Delete
Disconnect
Suspend
Revoke
Stop production resource
```

---

## 19. Crayon personality placement

The dashboard uses the established visual ratio:

```text
90% operational interface
10% crayon / grunge personality
```

Allowed accents:

```text
hand-drawn underline under one important heading
small marker arrow toward a critical action
occasional handwritten note
subtle paper texture
one expressive empty-state mark
```

Never apply crayon treatment to every card, chart, table row, or status badge.

---

## 20. Accessibility

The dashboard must remain understandable without color.

Status indicators use:

```text
icon + label + color
```

Never:

```text
color alone = state
```

Keyboard navigation must cover:

```text
Create menu
Dashboard cards
Tables
Row actions
Refresh controls
Command palette
```

Focus states must remain visible against the paper background.

---

## 21. Performance budget

The dashboard should prioritize useful content over animation.

Rules:

```text
No heavy decorative animation on initial load.
No client-side data waterfall when one API request can aggregate data.
No chart library for simple numeric indicators.
No polling faster than operationally necessary.
Lazy-load secondary visualizations.
```

The first meaningful operational state should appear before low-priority analytics.

---

## 22. Data contract expectation

The dashboard API should provide a purpose-built read model rather than forcing the client to fetch every resource independently.

Conceptual response:

```json
{
  "attention": [],
  "health": {},
  "deployments": [],
  "applications": [],
  "whatsapp": [],
  "automations": {},
  "usage": {},
  "activity": []
}
```

The exact API schema belongs to the implementation/domain layer and must remain aligned with the canonical resource model.

---

## 23. Acceptance criteria

The dashboard is ready for implementation when:

```text
[ ] A new user understands what to do next.
[ ] Running/failed/deploying states are immediately distinguishable.
[ ] Attention items appear before passive metrics.
[ ] Deployment status is visible without opening a detail page.
[ ] WhatsApp health is visible when instances exist.
[ ] Usage is understandable without reading documentation.
[ ] Partial API failures do not blank the entire page.
[ ] Empty/loading/error states are intentional.
[ ] Mobile order preserves operational priority.
[ ] Crayon styling never reduces readability.
[ ] No provider-specific terminology leaks into the default experience.
```

---

## 24. Implementation boundary

This showcase does **not** define:

```text
brand identity
navigation taxonomy
base design tokens
primitive component specifications
resource schemas
provider implementations
billing rules
```

Those are owned by the earlier source-of-truth documents.

This document only defines **how the authenticated Overview dashboard composes existing CRAYON primitives and resources into an operational experience**.
# CRAYON — UI Design System v1.0

## Status

**Version:** 1.0  
**Role:** Visual and interaction source of truth  
**Product:** CRAYON  
**Official meaning:** Create. Run. Automate. Your Own Network.  
**Core promise:** Make it run.

---

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the visual and interaction language for CRAYON. It exists to prevent one-off visual decisions across the landing page, application shell, dashboard, deployment surfaces, WhatsApp/Baileys surfaces, automation, observability, billing, and future modules.

CRAYON is a technical product with personality. The visual system must preserve usability first and express personality through controlled crayon/grunge details.

The governing principle is:

```text
CLEAN SYSTEM
     +
HUMAN MARKS
     +
TECHNICAL PRECISION
```

Target visual ratio:

```text
90% professional SaaS usability
10% crayon / grunge personality
```

---

## 2. Visual North Star

CRAYON should feel like a sophisticated infrastructure product that was designed on paper before becoming software.

Reference qualities:

```text
paper notebook
editorial typography
engineering blueprint
black ink
marker highlight
subtle imperfection
modern SaaS density
technical clarity
```

CRAYON must not feel like:

```text
children's software
cartoon UI
gaming UI
cyberpunk dashboard
neon-glass UI
generic blue enterprise SaaS
random doodle collage
```

---

## 3. Color Tokens

### Base

```text
--crayon-paper-0       #FCFBF7
--crayon-paper-1       #F5F2E9
--crayon-paper-2       #EBE7DC
--crayon-ink-900       #111111
--crayon-ink-700       #2A2A2A
--crayon-ink-500       #68655F
--crayon-ink-300       #AAA69D
--crayon-white         #FFFFFF
```

### Accent

```text
--crayon-lime          #7CFF4F
--crayon-cyan          #00F0FF
--crayon-magenta       #FF2DFE
--crayon-orange        #FF9D00
```

### Semantic status

```text
--status-success       #35C759
--status-warning       #F5A623
--status-danger        #FF3B30
--status-info          #2F80ED
--status-neutral       #8E8E93
```

Accent colors are intentional highlights. Never make an entire screen neon.

---

## 4. Color Rules

1. Ink is the default text and structural color.
2. Paper is the default application canvas.
3. White is used for elevated surfaces where needed.
4. Lime/cyan/magenta/orange identify emphasis, not decoration.
5. Semantic status colors must remain semantically stable.
6. Do not use accent colors for unrelated actions merely because they look attractive.
7. A page should normally have one dominant accent and at most two supporting accents.

Example:

```text
Dashboard → mostly ink/paper + lime status accents
AI        → mostly ink/paper + magenta accent
Network   → mostly ink/paper + cyan accent
Billing   → mostly ink/paper + orange accent
```

---

## 5. Typography

Typography should combine a highly readable modern sans with a technical monospace layer.

### Primary UI

Use a modern grotesk/sans-serif for navigation, labels, forms, cards, and body text.

Preferred characteristics:

```text
clean
slightly condensed where useful
high x-height
excellent numeric clarity
```

### Technical UI

Use a monospace face for:

```text
logs
IDs
URLs
commands
environment variables
runtime details
resource metrics
code
build output
```

### Handwritten layer

Handwritten/crayon typography is only for:

```text
annotations
micro-copy
section accents
marketing moments
easter eggs
```

Never use it for:

```text
forms
critical status
error messages
billing values
navigation
long paragraphs
```

---

## 6. Type Scale

Reference scale:

```text
Display     64 / 1.00 / 700
Hero        48 / 1.05 / 700
H1          36 / 1.10 / 700
H2          28 / 1.15 / 700
H3          22 / 1.20 / 650
Title       18 / 1.25 / 650
Body        15 / 1.50 / 450
Small       13 / 1.40 / 500
Caption     11 / 1.30 / 600
Mono        13 / 1.45 / 450
```

Do not create arbitrary font sizes for individual screens.

---

## 7. Spacing

Use an 8-point base grid:

```text
4   micro
8   tight
12  compact
16  standard
24  section
32  major
48  large
64  hero
96  marketing
```

Use spacing to create hierarchy instead of excessive borders or shadows.

---

## 8. Radius

The product should be friendly without becoming toy-like.

```text
radius-xs      4px
radius-sm      8px
radius-md      12px
radius-lg      16px
radius-xl      24px
radius-pill    999px
```

Default controls: 8–12px.  
Default cards: 12–16px.  
Hero/marketing panels may use larger radii.

---

## 9. Borders

Borders are part of the CRAYON identity.

Default:

```text
1px solid rgba(17,17,17,.14)
```

Strong structural border:

```text
1px solid #111111
```

Hand-drawn border is reserved for selected marketing cards, empty states, callouts, or special feature moments.

Never use intentionally irregular borders for dense operational tables.

---

## 10. Shadows

Use extremely restrained shadows.

Preferred:

```text
0 1px 2px rgba(17,17,17,.06)
0 8px 24px rgba(17,17,17,.08)
```

The visual hierarchy should come primarily from spacing, borders, and surface contrast.

---

## 11. Crayon Language

Crayon effects are a semantic visual layer, not a component theme applied everywhere.

### Allowed motifs

```text
marker underline
scribbled circle
hand-drawn arrow
highlight stroke
small handwritten note
rough-edged label
imperfect accent line
```

### Density rules

Operational screens:

```text
0–2 crayon accents per viewport
```

Marketing screens:

```text
2–6 accents per viewport
```

Never cover content with doodles.

### Semantic meaning

A crayon mark should do one job:

```text
call attention
explain a relationship
show personality
highlight a CTA
```

If removing the mark improves clarity, remove it.

---

## 12. Grunge Layer

Grunge is texture, not visual noise.

Allowed:

```text
very subtle paper grain
slight ink imperfections
controlled distressed texture
small offset marker marks
```

Avoid:

```text
heavy film grain
scratches across text
dirty backgrounds
high-contrast noise
random texture overlays
```

Accessibility and readability always win over texture.

---

## 13. Application Shell

Desktop shell:

```text
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ CRAYON                         Search         Account        │
├───────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│               │                                            │
│ Overview      │                                            │
│               │                                            │
│ CREATE        │               CONTENT AREA                 │
│ Applications  │                                            │
│ Templates     │                                            │
│ Projects      │                                            │
│               │                                            │
│ RUN           │                                            │
│ Deployments   │                                            │
│ Containers    │                                            │
│ Workers       │                                            │
│ Jobs          │                                            │
│               │                                            │
│ AUTOMATE      │                                            │
│ Workflows     │                                            │
│ AI Agents     │                                            │
│ WhatsApp      │                                            │
│               │                                            │
│ NETWORK       │                                            │
│ Domains       │                                            │
│ Storage       │                                            │
│ Data          │                                            │
│ Secrets       │                                            │
│               │                                            │
│ OBSERVE       │                                            │
│ Logs          │                                            │
│ Metrics       │                                            │
│ Usage         │                                            │
│               │                                            │
│ ACCOUNT       │                                            │
│ Billing       │                                            │
│ Team          │                                            │
│ Settings      │                                            │
└───────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The sidebar must remain compact. Group labels are small, high-contrast, and visually secondary.

---

## 14. Navigation Rules

Navigation should follow intent:

```text
CREATE
RUN
AUTOMATE
NETWORK
OBSERVE
ACCOUNT
```

Do not expose provider names such as “Cloudflare Worker” as top-level navigation.

Advanced provider details belong inside runtime configuration.

---

## 15. Dashboard Grammar

The dashboard answers six questions in order:

```text
1. What needs attention?
2. What's running?
3. What changed?
4. What's deploying?
5. How much am I using?
6. What should I do next?
```

Recommended structure:

```text
Page header
  ↓
Attention / incident strip
  ↓
Health snapshot
  ↓
Applications + deployments
  ↓
WhatsApp / automation status
  ↓
Usage
  ↓
Recent events
```

Avoid chart walls.

---

## 16. Cards

Cards should be used to group meaning, not to turn every field into a floating rectangle.

Card anatomy:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│ title                      action    │
│ supporting context                   │
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                      │
│ primary content                      │
│                                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Prefer 1 strong metric + context over 8 tiny metrics.

---

## 17. Buttons

### Primary

Strong ink button or selected accent fill depending on context.

Use for:

```text
Deploy
Create
Start
Save
Connect
```

### Secondary

Outlined/soft surface.

Use for:

```text
Cancel
Preview
Open logs
Configure
```

### Destructive

Red semantic treatment. Never use a crayon accent as destructive shorthand.

### Icon-only

Only when the icon meaning is standard or has an accessible tooltip.

---

## 18. Status System

Statuses must be readable without relying on color alone.

Recommended pattern:

```text
● RUNNING
● BUILDING
● DEPLOYING
● DEGRADED
● FAILED
● STOPPED
```

Combine:

```text
indicator + label + optional context
```

Never communicate critical state through color alone.

---

## 19. Forms

Form design should be calm and predictable.

Rules:

```text
label above field
help text below field
required state explicit
error state local
one primary submit action
```

Avoid custom hand-drawn controls for ordinary forms.

Use crayon annotation only to explain unusual concepts.

---

## 20. Tables

Tables are a core infrastructure surface.

Principles:

```text
compact rows
clear column labels
right-align numbers
monospace IDs when useful
sticky header for long lists
status visible in first few columns
bulk actions only when relevant
```

Do not use crayon textures inside dense tables.

---

## 21. Deployment Surface

A deployment page should visually prioritize:

```text
Application
Current status
Version / commit
Runtime
Region
Started at
Duration
Primary URL
```

Lifecycle:

```text
QUEUED
  ↓
BUILDING
  ↓
RELEASING
  ↓
STARTING
  ↓
HEALTHY
```

Failure state:

```text
FAILED
  ↓
reason
  ↓
relevant log excerpt
  ↓
likely fix
  ↓
RETRY
```

---

## 22. WhatsApp / Baileys Surface

The WhatsApp experience gets a distinctive but restrained CRAYON treatment.

Primary card:

```text
WA-SALES-01
● CONNECTED

Messages       12,492
Automations       37
AI                    ON
Uptime            7d 12h
```

Pairing surface:

```text
┌───────────────────────┐
│                       │
│          QR           │
│                       │
└───────────────────────┘

scan → connect → go
```

Use a handwritten annotation near the QR area, not around every field.

---

## 23. Terminal / Logs

Logs are intentionally more technical than the rest of the application.

```text
background → near-black ink
text       → paper-white
accent     → one contextual accent
font       → monospace
```

Provide:

```text
filter
search
copy
follow
pause
clear
severity
request / deployment context
```

Do not make logs look handwritten.

---

## 24. Empty States

Empty states can carry more CRAYON personality.

Recommended anatomy:

```text
small illustration / mark
clear statement
short explanation
one primary action
optional handwritten note
```

Example:

```text
Nothing here yet.

Your first app is waiting.

[ Create application ]

↳ let's make something.
```

---

## 25. Error States

Error UI must be serious and actionable.

```text
WHAT HAPPENED
WHY IT MATTERS
WHAT YOU CAN DO
```

Example:

```text
Deployment failed.

The health check did not receive a response.

[ View logs ] [ Retry ]
```

Never joke about security failures, billing failures, or irreversible destructive errors.

---

## 26. Command Palette

Command palette is a first-class CRAYON primitive.

Structure:

```text
┌────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Search or run a command…              │
├────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ CREATE                                 │
│   New application                      │
│   New workflow                         │
│   New WhatsApp                         │
│                                         │
│ RUN                                    │
│   Deploy project                       │
│   Restart container                    │
│   View logs                            │
└────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Safe actions may execute immediately. Destructive actions require confirmation.

---

## 27. Motion

Motion should communicate state, not decoration.

Use:

```text
150–200ms → micro interactions
200–300ms → panels / menus
300–450ms → larger transitions
```

Prefer subtle:

```text
fade
slide
scale 0.98 → 1
progress
status pulse
```

Avoid constant motion, bouncing UI, and decorative confetti.

---

## 28. Responsiveness

### Desktop
Primary design target for infrastructure operations.

### Tablet
Collapse secondary navigation and preserve primary actions.

### Mobile
Prioritize:

```text
status
primary action
recent activity
critical alerts
```

Do not simply shrink desktop tables into unreadable layouts. Convert dense data to cards or horizontal scroll when appropriate.

---

## 29. Accessibility

Minimum requirements:

```text
keyboard navigable
visible focus
semantic labels
color-independent status
sufficient contrast
reduced motion support
accessible dialogs
accessible tables
```

Crayon and grunge effects must never lower readability or contrast.

---

## 30. Responsive + Themed Surfaces

Use a single semantic token layer so future dark mode or alternate themes can be introduced without rewriting component contracts.

Components should consume semantic variables rather than hard-coded hex values.

Example:

```css
--surface-app
--surface-panel
--surface-raised
--text-primary
--text-secondary
--border-default
--accent-primary
--status-success
--status-danger
```

---

## 31. Component Inventory

### Foundations

```text
Color
Typography
Spacing
Radius
Border
Shadow
Icon
Motion
```

### Core

```text
Button
Input
Select
Textarea
Checkbox
Radio
Switch
Badge
Status
Tooltip
Popover
Dropdown
Dialog
Tabs
Toast
Pagination
```

### Data

```text
Card
Metric
Table
List
Timeline
Chart
Activity
LogViewer
CodeBlock
```

### CRAYON-specific

```text
CrayonUnderline
CrayonArrow
CrayonHighlight
CrayonNote
CrayonSticker
InkBorder
PaperSurface
BlueprintGrid
```

CRAYON-specific primitives must remain optional overlays, never mandatory wrappers around every component.

---

## 32. Page Templates

Standard page families:

```text
Dashboard
List
Detail
Create
Edit
Wizard
Workspace
Terminal
Settings
Billing
Empty
Error
```

Each page family must reuse the same header/action grammar.

---

## 33. Detail Page Grammar

```text
[ Back ]

Resource Name                     [status] [primary action]
Resource type · identifier

────────────────────────────────────────────
Overview | Activity | Configuration | Usage
────────────────────────────────────────────

Primary content

Secondary context
```

The first screenful must answer what the resource is and whether it is healthy.

---

## 34. Marketing vs Product

### Marketing

Can use:

```text
larger type
more crayon marks
more expressive composition
more texture
more asymmetry
```

### Product

Must prioritize:

```text
clarity
predictability
density
status
actionability
accessibility
```

### Developer surfaces

Can increase:

```text
monospace
grid
terminal aesthetics
technical annotations
```

---

## 35. Copy Rules

CRAYON copy should be:

```text
short
clear
confident
human
occasionally playful
```

Examples:

```text
Deploying…
Yep. It's alive.
Ship it.
Nothing here yet.
It died. We brought it back.
Connected.
Make it run.
```

Do not make system copy vague merely to sound cool.

---

## 36. Design Do / Don't

### DO

```text
use whitespace
use strong typography
use semantic status
use restrained accents
use occasional handwritten notes
use consistent component grammar
use real technical information
```

### DON'T

```text
turn everything into stickers
make all borders irregular
use gradients everywhere
hide status behind decoration
use fake metrics
make critical UI playful
recreate generic Vercel/Linear UI without CRAYON personality
```

---

## 37. Definition of Done for a Screen

A screen is not considered complete until:

```text
[ ] information hierarchy is obvious
[ ] primary action is obvious
[ ] loading state exists
[ ] empty state exists
[ ] error state exists
[ ] destructive state has confirmation
[ ] mobile behavior is defined
[ ] keyboard behavior is defined
[ ] status is color-independent
[ ] crayon accents are intentional
[ ] tokens are used instead of arbitrary values
[ ] reusable components are used
```

---

## 38. Implementation Contract

Every frontend implementation must treat this document as a design contract.

Do not introduce:

```text
one-off colors
arbitrary radii
random font sizes
untracked shadows
unreviewed doodles
provider-specific navigation labels
```

New visual primitives must either reuse existing tokens or be explicitly added to this system.

---

## 39. Next Visual Deliverables

The next design artifacts should be produced in this order:

```text
01  Logo / wordmark direction
02  Color token implementation
03  Typography implementation
04  Iconography rules
05  Core component showcase
06  Application shell
07  Dashboard
08  Application detail
09  Deployment detail
10  WhatsApp / Baileys workspace
11  Automation builder
12  Logs / metrics
13  Billing
14  Landing page
```

The component showcase becomes the visual regression reference for the entire product.

---

## 40. Final Principle

CRAYON should never ask:

> “How do we make this look more like a crayon?”

The better question is:

> “Where would a human mark this interface to make the important thing easier to see?”

That is the visual language.

```text
CRAYON

clean enough to trust.

weird enough to remember.
```